<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurukshetra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kurukshetra is a city and district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition it is the battlefield of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> war and the place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, and its sacred tanks, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> Sarovar, draw pilgrims. The town of Thanesar, capital of the 7th-century king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, lies within it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurukshetra.jpg" alt="Kurukshetra" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sid Sharma5911 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukshetra_junction_kkde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kurukshetra is a historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, also called Dharmakshetra, the realm of duty, and the Land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>. The name also refers to the city of Kurukshetra, the administrative headquarters of Kurukshetra district. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> it is described as a region, not a city, and kshetra means region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. Its boundaries correspond roughly to the central and western parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and one account places it between two rivers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a> and the Drishadvati.</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, the region is named after King Kuru, ancestor of the Kauravas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, and the Kurukshetra War of that epic is believed to have taken place here. The Vamana Purana says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> blessed Kuru with two boons: that the land would be known as holy after his name, and that anyone dying there would go to heaven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a>, a union of Vedic Indo-Aryan tribes of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> (c. 1200 to c. 900 BCE), developed into the first recorded state-level society in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. It collected its ritual hymns into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and developed the Srauta rituals, which contributed to the roots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. It dominated the middle Vedic period under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parikshit">Parikshit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janamejaya">Janamejaya</a>, but had become something of a backwater by the 5th century BCE. Its traditions supplied the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>.</p><p>The Mauryan empire conquered Kurukshetra in the late 4th century BCE, after which it became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. The Kushans later took the region, and the Gupta empire conquered it in the early 4th century CE, when it saw a cultural and religious revival. The Pushyabhuti dynasty ruled after the Gupta empire, and the town reached its height under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> (c. 590 to 647 CE), when the Chinese scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited Thanesar. Civil war followed Harsha's death without a successor.</p><p>Tomara A Kashmiri army briefly held the area in 733, and the Tomara dynasty took over from 736. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> sacked it in 1014, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> in 1206. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> put down a local rebellion in 1526 and made it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> it became a spiritual centre for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> and Muslims. Maratha forces controlled it until the British took it in 1805, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kurukshetra is an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage destination. The 48 kos parikrama is a traditional circuit of about 90 km round the holy city, made on foot to all its sites. Brahma Sarovar, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>'s largest man-made ponds, draws many pilgrims to bathe on Somavati Amavasya and at solar eclipses, and Jyotisar is the site where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a> is said to have been delivered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kurukshetra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Bhagwanpura in the <b>Kurukshetra</b> district of Haryana, large houses may have been the homes of an elite.</blockquote><a href="/culture/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>A much older chronology comes from post-Vedic Hindu texts such as the Mahabharata, the Ramayana and the Puranas, in which the Vedas were received by the seven rishis thousands of years ago and the <b>Kurukshetra</b> War is dated to about 3100 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gita is a dialogue between the Pandava prince Arjuna and his charioteer Krishna, whom Hindu tradition regards as an avatar of Vishnu, just before the <b>Kurukshetra</b> War.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The historicity of the <b>Kurukshetra</b> War is unclear; many historians date it to the 10th century BCE, and F. E. Pargiter's reckoning from Puranic genealogies gives about 950 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The British confiscated Ladwa in 1846, and its lands became part of a British district, now in <b>Kurukshetra</b> district of Haryana.</blockquote><a href="/state/ladwa-state">Ladwa State</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Sikhs captured Sirhind in 1763–64, Sikh sardars divided the surrounding country, and a Sikh family established itself at Thanesar, the old town beside the sacred tanks of <b>Kurukshetra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thanesar-state">Thanesar State</a></li></ul></section>
